From cf163c91c7c15a18b2dfc2d96320ced05e2380ba Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Fountain5405 <Fountain5405@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 03:34:09 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 19/27] ringct: verify a block's BP+ batch as sub-batches over
 the compute pool

Why: after the sum checks, verRctSemanticsSimple verifies all of a block's
BP+ proofs as one batch on the calling thread. On a block of about 1,300
proofs that takes about 0.7 s on a Ryzen 9 3900X core and about 2.5 s on a
Core 2 Quad, with the pool idle.

verBulletproofPlusSubBatches(proofs, n) splits the proofs round-robin into
n batches and verifies them in parallel; it accepts iff every batch does.
verRctSemanticsSimple uses one sub-batch per pool thread, at most one per
physical core (the work is compute-bound and SMT siblings slow it, the rule
batchVerifyFcmpPpProofs already follows), and at least 8 proofs per
sub-batch. Below 16 proofs it is the single batch, as before.

Safety:
- Accept/reject is unchanged. A batch accepts only if every proof in it
  verifies, and bulletproof_plus_VERIFY draws a fresh random weight per
  proof inside each batch, so each proof is weighted independently whatever
  batch it is in. Only the generator sums are shared per sub-batch instead
  of per block.
- An exception in a sub-batch fails it.
- Verification only; public data.

Tests: ringct.bpp_sub_batches_accept_valid (64 proofs as 0, 1, 2, 3, 8, 64
and 100 sub-batches; a single proof), and
ringct.bpp_sub_batches_reject_one_bad_proof (a corrupted proof at 0, 5, 8,
37 and 63 of 64, for 1, 2, 3, 8 and 64 sub-batches, and through
verRctSemanticsSimple).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
